    "raw": "Given:\n- The first tank is 300 liters filled.\n- The second tank is 450 liters filled and is 45% full.\n- Both tanks have the same capacity.\n\nFirst, let's find the capacity of the second tank. Since 450 liters is 45% of the tank's capacity, we can set up the equation:\n\\[ 0.45 \\times \\text{capacity} = 450 \\]\n\\[ \\text{capacity} = \\frac{450}{0.45} = 1000 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nSince both tanks have the same capacity, the first tank also has a capacity of 1000 liters.\n\nNext, we calculate how much more water is needed to fill each tank:\n- For the first tank: \\( 1000 - 300 = 700 \\) liters.\n- For the second tank: \\( 1000 - 450 = 550 \\) liters.\n\nAdding these together gives the total amount of water needed:\n\\[ 700 + 550 = 1250 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nFINAL: 1250",
